# Findings the release scan should not block on.
#
# `.github/workflows/release-image.yml` gates every image on CRITICAL and HIGH, fixed or not,
# plus any secret found in a layer. That is strict on purpose: a base-image CVE should stop a
# release rather than ship quietly. This file is the valve, and an entry here is a decision, not
# a mute button.
#
# One CVE id per line. Write the reason above it and an expiry date, so a line that outlived its
# reason is visible as an old date rather than as a line nobody remembers adding:
#
#   # CVE-2025-0000 — in libfoo, reachable only from the CLI's --serve mode, which no image runs.
#   # Revisit 2026-12-01, or when debian ships the fix.
#   CVE-2025-0000
#
# Empty is the goal. A growing file means the base image needs bumping, not that the gate is
# wrong.

# The below is inside npm's OWN vendored tree in the two executor images, which keep npm because
# Claude Code's plugin path uses it. npm 11.21.1 is the newest release on the 11 line and still
# carries it; so does npm 12.2.1, which also breaks the build — @anthropic-ai/claude-code's
# postinstall does not place its native binary under npm 12, and `claude plugin install` then fails
# with "claude native binary not installed". So there is no npm to upgrade to, and the alternative
# (removing npm from the executors) takes the plugin path with it.
# It is not reachable from anything these images run: npm is a build-time tool there, and no
# runner invokes it.

# undici denial of service via an unhandled stream error. npm 11.21.1 vendors 6.29.0; the fix is
# 6.29.1. Revisit 2026-11-24, or when an npm release vendors undici 6.29.1.
CVE-2026-109869
